Topic 2: Django ORM Methods
1. What is ORM?
ORM stands for Object-Relational Mapping.

Django ORM allows us to interact with a database using Python code instead of writing SQL queries manually.

For example, instead of writing SQL:
SELECT * FROM product WHERE price > 1000;


We can write Python:
Product.objects.filter(price__gt=1000)



Both retrieve products whose price is greater than 1000.
2. Important ORM methods for interviews
Assume we have this model:
class Product(models.Model):    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)    price = models.IntegerField()    stock = models.IntegerField(default=0)



Method	Purpose	Example
all()	Get all records	Product.objects.all()
get()	Get one matching record	Product.objects.get(id=1)
filter()	Get matching records	Product.objects.filter(price=500)
exclude()	Get records that don't match	Product.objects.exclude(price=500)
create()	Insert a record	Product.objects.create(name="Phone", price=20000)
update()	Update matching records	Product.objects.filter(id=1).update(price=25000)
delete()	Delete matching records	Product.objects.filter(id=1).delete()
order_by()	Sort records	Product.objects.order_by("price")
count()	Count matching records	Product.objects.count()
exists()	Check whether records exist	Product.objects.filter(price=500).exists()
first()	Get the first result or None	Product.objects.all().first()
last()	Get the last result or None	Product.objects.all().last()

Important differences
get() vs filter()
- get() returns one object. It raises an exception if no object matches or multiple objects match.
- filter() returns a QuerySet, even if no records match.
save() vs update()
product = Product.objects.get(id=1)product.price = 30000product.save()



This modifies a model instance and saves it.
Product.objects.filter(id=1).update(price=30000)



This updates matching rows directly in the database without calling each model instance's save() method.
3. Field lookups
Field lookups help you filter records based on conditions.
Lookup	Meaning	Example
exact	Exact match	filter(name__exact="Phone")
gt	Greater than	filter(price__gt=1000)
gte	Greater than or equal	filter(price__gte=1000)
lt	Less than	filter(price__lt=1000)
lte	Less than or equal	filter(price__lte=1000)
contains	Contains text	filter(name__contains="Phone")
icontains	Case-insensitive contains	filter(name__icontains="phone")
in	Matches any value in a collection	filter(id__in=[1, 2, 3])

For example:
Product.objects.filter(price__gte=1000)



Returns products priced at 1000 or more.
